# Riff Web — Cursor Rules

You are editing the Riff marketing website. This document is your operating
manual. Read it before every change. Treat its rules as binding.

## What this project is

A single-page marketing site for **Riff**, a payments/invoicing/splits tool
for creator collaborators. Built in Next.js 14 (App Router) + TypeScript +
Tailwind + shadcn/ui patterns.

The site is built in the **Swiss International Style (Editorial register)**,
with a single brand-spectrum gradient applied via the **Ghost Border**
philosophy — gradient ONLY appears as borders on signature elements, never
as fills, never as text.

For deeper context, read:
- `docs/IA.md` — full information architecture, section purposes, intended
  pages, content hierarchy
- `README.md` — file structure, run instructions

## Hard design rules — NEVER violate these

1. **No rounded corners. Anywhere. Ever.** All borders are sharp 90° angles.
   `rounded-none` is the default. Never use `rounded-md`, `rounded-lg`, etc.
   The one exception is `.rounded-full` for the circle in the hero Bauhaus
   composition — that's a deliberate geometric primitive, not UI ornamentation.
2. **No drop shadows.** No `shadow-sm`, `shadow-md`, `shadow-xl`. Depth comes
   from texture patterns and visible borders, never from shadow.
3. **Borders are 2–4px thick.** Default to `border-[3px]`. Never thinner than
   2px. Always `border-swiss-ink` (warm dark gray) unless on a dark background.
4. **The palette is exactly four colors plus one gradient:**
   - `swiss-paper` (#F5F6F6, warm off-white) — the canvas
   - `swiss-ink` (#313131, warm dark gray) — text, borders, fills
   - `swiss-muted` (#EBECEC, light gray) — secondary surfaces
   - `swiss-focus` (#83EFC5, mint) — focus rings ONLY (accessibility)
   - `swiss-spectrum` (#FFB7F8 pink → #C2D3FF blue → #83EFC5 mint, 135deg) — see Ghost Border below
5. **Ghost Border philosophy.** The brand spectrum gradient is applied ONLY
   as a stroke around signature elements via `.swiss-spectrum-border`.
   NEVER as a fill. NEVER as text color. NEVER on more than 4 elements per
   page. Current sanctioned uses:
   - The hero Bauhaus square (1 instance per page)
   - The primary CTA button border (`prismatic` variant — repeats per section)
   - The featured pricing tier border (1 instance)
   - Form input focus rings (when added)
6. **Typography is Inter, all weights up to 900.** Headlines are uppercase.
   Body is mixed case. Section labels are uppercase with `tracking-[0.2em]`.
   Use the `text-display-sm/md/lg` scale for hero/section headlines.
7. **Outlined display type** (`.swiss-text-outline`) is reserved for hero
   headlines only. Use sparingly — typically one outlined word per line, or
   a deliberate fill/stroke hierarchy across multiple lines.
8. **No emoji, no decorative icons, no playful glyphs.** Use `lucide-react`
   icons only when they communicate meaning, not decoration.
9. **Hover states use color inversion**, not opacity fades. Black → paper,
   white → ink, etc. Always `duration-150 ease-linear` (instant, mechanical).
10. **No glass morphism, neumorphism, gradients-as-fill, or soft transitions.**

## Token system — change once, ripple everywhere

Design tokens live in TWO files that mirror each other:
- `app/globals.css` — CSS custom properties (`--swiss-paper`, etc.)
- `tailwind.config.ts` — Tailwind utilities (`swiss-paper`, etc.)

**If you change a color, font, or scale in one, update the other in the same
commit.** Never hardcode hex colors in components — always use the Swiss
tokens (`bg-swiss-ink`, `text-swiss-ink`).

The gradient is exposed two ways:
- As a `backgroundImage.spectrum` Tailwind utility → `bg-spectrum`
- As the `.swiss-spectrum-border` utility class → for border-image use

## Button variants

Defined in `components/ui/button.tsx`:

- **`primary`** — Solid ink fill, paper text. Hover inverts to paper fill +
  ink text. Use for general CTAs.
- **`secondary`** — Paper fill, thick ink border. Hover inverts. Use for
  ancillary actions next to a primary.
- **`prismatic`** — Paper fill, gradient (Ghost Border) border, ink text.
  Hover fills with ink (border stays gradient). Use for the SINGLE most
  important CTA in any section. This is the brand's signature button.
- **`ghost`** — Transparent, hover underlines. Use for tertiary nav-style
  actions or "I already have an account"-style links.

When adding a new button to a section, ask: "Is this THE primary call to
action of this entire section?" If yes → `prismatic`. If no → `primary`
or `secondary`.

## File structure conventions

- **One section per file** in `components/sections/`. Don't combine sections.
- **Reusable layout helpers** in `components/primitives/` (Container,
  SectionLabel, Rule).
- **shadcn-style UI primitives** in `components/ui/` (Button, future Input,
  Card, etc.). Always extend with `cva` variants — match the existing pattern
  in `components/ui/button.tsx`.
- **Page composition** lives ONLY in `app/page.tsx`. Pages should be thin —
  just imports and JSX composition. No logic.
- **Every section** must:
  - Be wrapped in `<Container>`
  - End with `border-b-[3px] border-swiss-ink` (so the page reads as a
    stack of horizontal bands)
  - Use a `<SectionLabel number="0X">Label</SectionLabel>` to anchor it
  - Have a massive uppercase H2 using `text-display-sm` or larger

## Voice — hybrid

- **Structural elements** (section labels, nav, button labels, table
  headers, footnotes): sober, declarative, uppercase. "01 — METHOD",
  "EVIDENCE", "PRICING".
- **Body copy and CTAs**: warm, conversational, slightly playful. Keep
  Riff's personality intact: "Start riffing — free", "Splits without
  spreadsheets", "April never sneaks up on you again."
- Never write generic SaaS copy. Never write "Empower your workflow."

## Texture utilities

Defined in `app/globals.css`. Apply only on muted-gray or paper surfaces,
never on ink or gradient surfaces:
- `.swiss-grid-pattern` — 24×24 grid lines (use on hero sidebar, feature backgrounds)
- `.swiss-dots` — 16×16 dot matrix (use on data viz containers, accent panels)
- `.swiss-diagonal` — 45° stripes (use sparingly for directional energy)
- `.swiss-noise` — fractal noise overlay (use on cards needing extra grain)

## Evolving the system itself

These rules govern component code. If you need to evolve the system itself
(add a color, change the gradient, allow rounded corners), the legitimate
path is:

1. Update the token in BOTH `app/globals.css` AND `tailwind.config.ts`
2. Update this file (`.cursorrules`) and `docs/IA.md` to reflect the new
   reality
3. The user must explicitly ask for the system change — don't propose
   evolutions inside an unrelated edit.

## Brand gradient — immutable sequence

The spectrum gradient MUST always follow this exact stop order:

```
linear-gradient(135deg, #FFB7F8 0%, #C2D3FF 50%, #83EFC5 100%)
```

Pink `#FFB7F8` → Blue `#C2D3FF` → Mint `#83EFC5`. Do not invert. Do not
randomize. Do not reorder stops. Do not change the angle without explicit
approval. This sequence is mirrored in `--swiss-spectrum` (globals.css) and
`backgroundImage.spectrum` (tailwind.config.ts) — if you change one, change
both in the same commit.

Any component that renders the gradient (prismatic button border, Bauhaus
square border, feature icon hover fill) inherits this sequence via the token.
Never hardcode gradient hex values in components — always reference the token.

## Marquee / loop animations — structural rule

Any CSS animation that is intended to scroll or loop continuously (e.g. the
agencies proof-bar marquee) MUST declare its sub-properties individually
rather than collapsing them into the `animation` shorthand. The reason: a
shorthand declaration (including `animation: none` from a base/reset layer)
can silently override the `iteration-count` that was set inside the same
shorthand. Explicit sub-properties survive such cascades unless targeted
directly.

**Required pattern for every infinite marquee:**

```css
.my-marquee {
  animation-name: my-keyframe;
  animation-duration: Xs;
  animation-timing-function: linear;
  animation-iteration-count: infinite;   /* NEVER collapse this into shorthand */
  animation-fill-mode: none;
  animation-play-state: running;
  will-change: transform;
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .my-marquee { animation: none; }
}
```

Never write `animation: my-keyframe Xs linear infinite` for a loop. The
shorthand is legal CSS but fragile under cascade. The explicit form is
mandatory here.

## When in doubt

- If a change might violate Swiss style or Ghost Border discipline,
  **ask before editing**.
- If a new component doesn't exist in `components/ui/`, build it following
  the Button pattern (cva + asChild + variant + size).
- If you need a new color, you don't. Use what exists.
- If you need to use the gradient in a new way, ask first.
- If you need rounded corners on UI elements, you don't. The answer is no.
